<h3>By the end of this unit you should be able to:</h3>
<p>•&nbsp; Explain what Sartre means by “existence precedes essence” and why he takes this to follow from atheism</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Describe the distinction between the <em>in-itself</em> (things) and the <em>for-itself</em> (consciousness), and explain why it matters for his account of freedom</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Explain Sartre’s three existential moods — anguish, abandonment, and despair — and what each one names</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Define bad faith and give at least one concrete example of how it works</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Reconstruct Sartre’s argument for the claim that existentialism is a humanism, not a counsel of despair</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Identify the tension between Sartre’s denial of objective moral norms and his apparent moral condemnation of bad faith</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Situate Sartre’s position in the arc of the course: how does he respond to Hegel’s historicism and Marx’s materialism?</p>
